    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nVolkswagen Apparently Makes Just €12 Per Vehicle While Mercedes Earns €4,198\n27 photos\nPhoto: Volkswagen\nA carmaker that sells nearly six million vehicles in the span of nine months should have more to show for it than 12 euros in operating earnings per car. That's where the Volkswagen Group ended up in the latest financial comparison from the Center of Automotive Management, which covers January 2026 through September 2026. The industry average, by comparison, stood at 1,363 euros.\nMercedes-Benz earned 4,198 euros per vehicle during this period, while BMW reached 3,059 euros. Why does the Wolfsburg-based manufacturer's result look this ugly? Part of the reason comes in the form of an €8.7 billion accounting burden. Without these special charges, the group's margin would have been 3.8 percent.\nThe CAM report examines 21 automotive manufacturers in total, with CAM using operating EBIT rather than net profit. In other words, the figures presented by the Center of Automotive Management should not be confused with the money automakers retain after every single expense, tax, and financing cost. Even with this distinction, Volkswagen's position is well-nigh impossible to defend.\nIts 12-euro figure puts the group near the bottom of the list, below Stellantis at 250 euros and three Chinese automakers. Geely, SAIC, and Great Wall purportedly generated between 704 and 683 euros. Looking over Volkswagen's own financial statements explains CAM's rather dramatic estimate.\nThe group posted 233.4 billion euros in revenue, down 2.2 percent from a year earlier, while vehicle sales fell 10.6 percent, from 6.58 to 5.88 million units. Operating earnings also collapsed, contracting from 5.41 billion euros to merely 71 million. That's a 98.7 percent decline, and this nosedive explains the CAM-estimated operating margin of approximately 0.03 percent.\nPhoto: Volkswagen Regarding those 8.7 billion euros in special items, including 7.8 in the third quarter alone, the biggest component is a non-cash impairment of Porsche goodwill totaling 6.4 billion euros. Another 1 billion comes from non-cash impairment adjustments on assets held by fully consolidated Chinese companies. Furthermore, additional expenses related to the Osnabruck plant's conversion into a defence manufacturing hub added to the burden.\nGoodwill impairments don't represent an equivalent cash payment during the reporting period. Instead, they reduce the accounting value of an asset when a given company reassesses the future economic benefits it expects from it. Even so, a theoretical 3.8 percent operating margin isn't particularly reassuring.\nRemember that Volkswagen is one of the biggest automotive groups out there, with Volkswagen also being Europe's largest automaker by sales. The group delivered 8.98 million new vehicles worldwide in 2025, down 0.5 percent from 2024. Per the group's Q3 2026 report, no fewer than 5,781,421 passenger and commercial vehicles were delivered globally in the first nine months of 2026.\nVolkswagen isn't the only troubled automaker here. CAM points out that Mercedes-Benz Cars suffered a 56 percent decline in operating EBIT, yet the wider group improved its operating margin from 4.4 to 5.5 percent. BMW, on the other hand, slipped by 2.5 percentage points to 5.6 percent due to weaker contributions from China and substantial investment requirements.\nWhat about Toyota , which is the world's largest automaker by sales? CAM estimates 1,857 euros per vehicle, down 20 percent, whereas Detroit-based General Motors reached 1,670 euros, up 29 percent. Tesla, which is dropping the Cybertruck in 2027, dropped 39 percent to 1,355 euros per vehicle.\nVia // auto-institut.de\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolkswagen Mercedes-Benz BMW Toyota Tesla industry business\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT This DIY Camper Van Is a Homey Mobile Abode Built for Cross-Country Adventures, It's Now for Sale",
